1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:02,540
This is GEONOS.

2
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:08,220
Select two buildings and speak the question you would take to a project meeting.

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:13,180
Muse Voice turns that speech into an editable draft.

4
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:18,220
Read it, refine the wording, and add it to your message.

5
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:26,090
Here, the question is practical: which checks matter when comparing these places for a climate-resilient

6
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:27,420
retrofit?

7
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:31,420
Send it with the selected buildings still in view.

8
00:00:33,488 --> 00:00:39,298
Muse Spark retrieves the selected official records and chooses the relevant evidence tools.

9
00:00:39,328 --> 00:00:45,908
The surrounding-ground tool follows each full building outline into the archived stormwater scenarios.

10
00:00:46,048 --> 00:00:53,698
Open Sources to inspect its results: the current sea-level scenario and the 2050 scenario, the distances to

11
00:00:53,728 --> 00:00:58,258
mapped water classes, and the areas in outside bands.

12
00:00:58,288 --> 00:01:05,218
The answer brings that geographic contrast back to your entrance, drainage, and equipment questions.

13
00:01:05,248 --> 00:01:10,628
Ask what to request from the owner, and the follow-up keeps both places in view.

14
00:01:12,697 --> 00:01:18,187
Both mapped scenarios use two point one three inches of rainfall in one hour.

15
00:01:18,217 --> 00:01:22,747
The published 2050 scenario adds thirty inches of sea level.

16
00:01:22,777 --> 00:01:32,587
At 572 Smith, the deep-water map class reaches the outline; at 592, it lies about forty-seven meters away.

17
00:01:32,617 --> 00:01:39,787
Inspect the same geographic extent, change the investigation band, and follow the recorded source date.

18
00:01:39,817 --> 00:01:47,547
That leads to a specific next visit: survey entrance thresholds, loading access, drainage paths, and equipment

19
00:01:47,577 --> 00:01:48,957
elevations.

20
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:53,770
Zoom out to New York City.

21
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:59,530
More than a million valid mapped footprints cover all five boroughs.

22
00:01:59,670 --> 00:02:04,040
Density gives way to individual outlines as you move closer.

23
00:02:04,070 --> 00:02:10,600
Choose a layer, read its legend, and explore recorded roof heights in three dimensions.

24
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:19,400
Search an address, building identifier, or tax-lot number; use filters or search the current view.

25
00:02:19,430 --> 00:02:24,650
Your selected A and B stay available while you explore other records.

26
00:02:26,655 --> 00:02:33,475
The left panel changes automatically from one building to a two-building comparison.

27
00:02:33,855 --> 00:02:38,515
These Smith Street outlines share one physical scale.

28
00:02:38,735 --> 00:02:48,245
Their mapped footprints are twenty-seven thousand one hundred ninety-three, and nineteen thousand two hundred

29
00:02:48,275 --> 00:02:49,875
fifty-nine square feet.

30
00:02:50,175 --> 00:02:56,115
Recorded roof height, construction year, and zoning have their own fields.

31
00:02:56,335 --> 00:03:04,705
Whole-tax-lot floor area carries a separate label, so you can follow each measurement back to the right

32
00:03:04,735 --> 00:03:08,115
official record and source revision.

33
00:03:10,197 --> 00:03:13,977
Choose the measurement that answers your immediate question.

34
00:03:14,437 --> 00:03:20,057
For this demonstration, require a mapped footprint of twenty thousand square feet.

35
00:03:20,517 --> 00:03:25,897
One candidate supports that condition; the other conflicts with it.

36
00:03:26,677 --> 00:03:30,137
Change the threshold and the comparison updates.

37
00:03:30,357 --> 00:03:40,647
Then choose the next operating fact to obtain: usable interior space, electrical capacity, or another explicit

38
00:03:40,677 --> 00:03:42,377
project requirement.

39
00:03:42,677 --> 00:03:49,417
Dated owner reports can sit alongside the public evidence, keeping the source of each answer visible.

40
00:03:51,488 --> 00:03:53,458
Ask for the visual comparison.

41
00:03:53,488 --> 00:04:01,778
GEONOS retrieves official Spring 2024 aerial crops at both buildings, and SAM 3.1 marks roof and tree image

42
00:04:01,808 --> 00:04:03,778
regions within those crops.

43
00:04:03,808 --> 00:04:08,818
Switch between A and B, inspect the overlays, and open each original image.

44
00:04:08,848 --> 00:04:13,298
Compare with map relates the detected roof regions to the recorded footprint.

45
00:04:13,328 --> 00:04:18,818
The surrounding band adds approximate context from those tree regions, giving the project team specific

46
00:04:18,848 --> 00:04:23,508
locations to examine in a roof, vegetation, and equipment survey.

47
00:04:25,572 --> 00:04:31,062
Muse Wonder reviews structured summaries from the completed image analyses alongside the public building

48
00:04:31,092 --> 00:04:32,152
records.

49
00:04:32,212 --> 00:04:39,542
Its review follows three lines of inquiry: roofs and equipment, tree context, and the relationship between the

50
00:04:39,572 --> 00:04:41,592
dated map and image.

51
00:04:41,812 --> 00:04:45,462
Open the source references behind each suggested survey.

52
00:04:45,492 --> 00:04:52,022
A separate second review can examine the current public-data comparison, name an assumption to investigate,

53
00:04:52,052 --> 00:04:55,672
and recommend the inspection that could change the decision.

54
00:04:57,738 --> 00:05:03,628
Now compare official 2020 and 2024 imagery at matching geographic bounds.

55
00:05:03,658 --> 00:05:10,108
DINOv2 runs locally in the browser and compares visual features in a sixteen-by-sixteen grid.

56
00:05:10,138 --> 00:05:15,948
Move the divider between dates, toggle the highlighted windows, and inspect the original photographs.

57
00:05:15,978 --> 00:05:21,548
This gives you another route to a focused question: which part of the site deserves a closer look,

58
00:05:21,578 --> 00:05:25,598
and which dated plan or field photograph would help explain it?

59
00:05:27,613 --> 00:05:31,583
Bring a planning requirement into local review with Muse Glimmer.

60
00:05:31,613 --> 00:05:35,663
Here, the team needs twenty thousand square feet of usable space.

61
00:05:35,693 --> 00:05:42,143
Glimmer quotes fresh public records, proposes the first owner request, and states a conditional next step: if

62
00:05:42,173 --> 00:05:47,823
measured usable area falls below that requirement, inspect the second site first.

63
00:05:47,853 --> 00:05:54,223
The review connects your own notes, a documented requirement, and the evidence behind each proposed action.

64
00:05:54,253 --> 00:05:57,633
Keep the notes and this review together on the computer.

65
00:05:59,697 --> 00:06:03,107
Climate evidence adds two useful scales.

66
00:06:03,137 --> 00:06:11,427
NOAA station history shows observed years, months, and metrics; published NPCC regional projections show

67
00:06:11,457 --> 00:06:14,277
future periods and their baselines.

68
00:06:14,337 --> 00:06:20,597
In the paired-weather view, temperature and dew point remain tied to the same observation and timestamp.

69
00:06:20,737 --> 00:06:27,987
Explore a hot period, inspect source coverage, and bring those paired conditions to the equipment discussion.

70
00:06:28,017 --> 00:06:33,477
Each view helps you specify the operating questions and measurements for the project horizon.

71
00:06:35,488 --> 00:06:40,708
For energy, open the separate example at 211 Lombardy Street.

72
00:06:40,848 --> 00:06:46,068
Trace the parent report, its linked buildings, and the child reports.

73
00:06:46,128 --> 00:06:53,588
Follow the floor area used in each energy-intensity calculation and inspect the source rows.

74
00:06:53,648 --> 00:07:01,698
That creates a concrete owner request: the meter-to-building schedule, the spaces served by each meter, and

75
00:07:01,728 --> 00:07:05,378
the operating uses covered by each report.

76
00:07:05,408 --> 00:07:09,668
The reporting boundary becomes part of the investigation.

77
00:07:11,655 --> 00:07:15,995
Muse Image turns the written checks into a conceptual inspection diagram.

78
00:07:16,215 --> 00:07:22,155
This earlier October thirtieth example uses 572 Smith and 611 Court.

79
00:07:22,215 --> 00:07:28,025
It connects what is known, the action to take, and the finding that could change the comparison.

80
00:07:28,055 --> 00:07:33,465
Enlarge the illustrated action rows and discuss the sequence with the owner or project team.

81
00:07:33,495 --> 00:07:38,155
The original written checks and their source references travel alongside the concept.

82
00:07:40,238 --> 00:07:47,008
The citywide workspace, evidence pipeline, and model integrations were built during this hackathon using

83
00:07:47,038 --> 00:07:53,058
official public datasets, existing pretrained models, and software libraries.

84
00:07:53,198 --> 00:08:01,328
Export the selected records and project conditions, then take the specific requests to the owner: roof plans,

85
00:08:01,358 --> 00:08:07,058
meter schedules, usable-space information, and electrical records.

86
00:08:07,198 --> 00:08:15,408
Seven Meta model roles connect a spoken question, dated public evidence, visual inspection, and local field

87
00:08:15,438 --> 00:08:18,578
notes into a better-informed next site visit.
